Docker 
1.What is Docker? 
Docker is an open source platform that makes it easy to build, package and run applications using containers.
2.What is hypervisor? 
A hypervisor is software that allows multiple virtual machines to run on one physical machine.
Type 1 — Bare-metal-Runs directly on the physical hardware. EX-Microsoft Hyper-V,
Type 2 — Hosted-Runs on top of an existing operating system. EX- VirtualBox.
3.What is virtualization? 
Virtualization means creating a virtual computer inside a physical computer. Every VM includes CPU, RAM , OS, Network interface.
4.what is containerisation? 
is an OS level virtualization method used to deploy and run distributed applications without launching an entire VM for each laptop. 
5.Difference between virtual Machine and containers?
A Virtual Machine runs a complete operating system using a hypervisor, so it is heavier and uses more resources.
A Container shares the host OS kernel and runs only the application and its dependencies, making it lightweight and faster
6.What is Docker container? 
Lightweight software environment. Running instances of images.

7.What is Docker image? 
Read only template or blueprint that used to create docker containers.
8.What is Docker registry?
A Docker Registry is a service that stores and distributes Docker images.

9.What is Docker repository?
A Docker repository is a place where Docker images are stored and managed,
10.What is Docker file? 
Docker file is a text file in which use the commands and create a customized container according to the requirements.
11.How will you remove an image from Docker? 
By using the command -- docker rmi <image-name or id>

12.Explain basic docker usage workflow? 
Pull/Build an image → docker pull <image> or docker build
Run a container → docker run <image>
Check containers → docker ps
Stop/Remove container → docker stop <container> / docker rm <container>
Remove image → docker rmi <image>

13.Explain docker architecture?
User gives a command through the Docker Client (docker run, docker build, etc.).
The Docker Daemon receives the request and manages Docker objects.
If the required image is not available locally, Docker pulls it from a Docker Registry like Docker Hub.
The Daemon uses the Docker Image to create and run a Container.
Docker Client sends commands to the Docker Daemon, which gets images from a Registry when required and uses those images to create and run containers.
14.How many containers can be created in one docker server?
There is no fixed limit on the number of containers in one Docker server. The number depends mainly on the CPU, RAM, storage, and workload of the server.
15.Can we delete image, if there is a running container for that image?
First stop and remove the container, then remove the image using docker rmi <image>.
16.what is dangling image? 
A dangling image is a Docker image that has no tag and is not associated with any container, usually left behind after rebuilding an image.
17.How do you copy image from one docker serves to another docker serves without using docker registry? 
We can transfer a Docker image without a registry by using docker save to export the image as a .tar file, copy it to the other server, and use docker load to import it.
docker save -o image.tar myimage:latest
scp image.tar user@server:/tmp/
docker load -i image.tar
18.What is the difference between docker create and docker run? 
docker create → Creates a container but does not start it.
docker run → Creates and starts the container in a single command.
19.What is the difference between docker stop and docker kill?
docker stop → stops the container, giving the application time to shut down properly.
docker kill → Immediately stops the container without giving it time to shut down or stopping it's execution immediately.
20.Can we do port binding mapping for an existing container if we have missed while creating a container?
No, you cannot add or change port mapping for an existing container.
We need to stop and remove the existing container, then create a new container with the required port mapping using -p.
21.How can you check the status of a container in Docker? 
docker ps - shows running containers 
docker ps -a  - shows both running and stopped containers
22.What is the difference between docker ps and docker Ps -a? 
docker ps shows running containers, whereas docker ps -a shows all containers.
23.What are the various states that a docker container can be? 
The main container states are Created, Running, Paused, Restarting, Exited.
Created – Container is created but not started.
Running – Container is currently running.
Paused – Container processes are temporarily paused.
Restarting – Container is being restarted.
Exited/Stopped – Container has stopped running.
24.your application is running as a container. And your application is not accessible. How will you troubleshoot?
First, I will check whether the container is running using docker ps. Then I will check the container logs using docker logs. Next, I will verify the port mapping using docker port or docker ps, check whether the application is listening on the correct port, and finally check network/firewall issues.
Check container status
        ↓
Check container logs
        ↓
Check port mapping
        ↓
Check application/service inside container
        ↓
Check Docker network
        ↓
Check firewall / security rules
25.can we have run more than one process inside a container? 
Yes, we can run multiple processes inside a Docker container, but it is generally recommended to run one main process per container.
Multiple processes are possible, but for better scalability and management, Docker follows the best practice of running one main application/process per container.
26.What is docker cp command? 
docker cp is used to copy files or directories between a Docker container and the host machine.
docker cp <container>:/path/file /host/path
docker cp /host/path/file <container>:/path/
27.What is the difference between copy and docker cp? 
COPY → Used in a Dockerfile to copy files from the build context into the Docker image while building it.
docker cp → Used to copy files between a running/stopped container and the host machine.

28.What is docker commit? 
docker commit is used to create a new Docker image from the current state of an existing container.
docker commit <container_name> <new_image_name>
29.What is the difference between ADD and COPY instructions in a Dockerfile? 
COPY is preferred for simple file/directory copying, while ADD provides additional features like tar extraction and URL support.
30.What is the difference between RUN and CMD instruction in Docker file?
RUN → Executes a command while building the Docker image and creates a new image layer.
CMD → Specifies the default command to run when the container starts.
31.can we have more than one CMD instruction in a dockerfile? 
A Docker file can contain multiple CMD instructions, but Docker executes only the last CMD instruction.
32.What is the difference between docker run command and RUN?
RUN is used during image building, while docker run is used to create and start a container
33.What is Build cache in Docker?
Docker build cache stores previously built image layers and reuses them during the next build if nothing has changed, making builds faster.
34.What is ENTRYPOINT in Dockerfile? 
ENTRYPOINT in a Dockerfile defines the main command or executable that runs when the container starts. It is generally not overridden by command-line arguments, unlike CMD, which provides default arguments or a default command.

35.What is the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT instruction in Dockerfile?
CMD → Provides the default command or arguments and can be easily overridden when running the container.
ENTRYPOINT → Defines the main executable for the container and is not easily overridden.
36.Usually Dockefile starts with 'FROM' instruction Can we use any other instruction before 'FROM' instruction?
We can use ARG before FROM to define build-time variables used in the FROM instruction.
37.What is the difference between ARG and ENV instructions in dockerfile?
ARG → Defines variables available only during the Docker image build.
ENV → Defines environment variables available during build and inside the running container.
38.What Is WORKDIR instruction in dockerfile? 
WORKDIR sets the default directory inside the container where commands will be executed.
39.What is the purpose of Expose command in Dockerfile?
EXPOSE documents the container port, but it does not actually publish the port to the host. Port publishing is done using -p with docker run.



40.What is .dockerignore file?
.dockerignore is a file used to exclude unnecessary files and directories from the Docker build context. 
41.What is multi stage docker file?
A multi-stage Dockerfile uses multiple FROM instructions to separate the build stage from the final runtime stage. 
42.What is working directory of docker?
The working directory in Docker is the directory inside the container where commands are executed by default. 
43.What is local registry for docker? 
A local registry is used to privately store and distribute Docker images without relying on a public registry like Docker Hub.
44.What are the instructions you used in dockerfile?
The commonly used Dockerfile instructions are FROM, RUN, COPY, ADD, WORKDIR, ENV, ARG, EXPOSE, CMD, ENTRYPOINT, USER, and LABEL.
FROM – Defines the base image.
RUN – Executes commands while building the image.
COPY – Copies files into the image.
ADD – Copies files and supports additional features like tar extraction.
WORKDIR – Sets the working directory.
ENV – Sets environment variables.
ARG – Defines build-time variables.
EXPOSE – Documents the application port.
CMD – Defines the default command.
ENTRYPOINT – Defines the main executable.
USER – Specifies the user to run commands/container.
LABEL – Adds metadata to the image.
